      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "59d4a47f"
      },
      "source": [
        "## Summary:\n",
        "\n",
        "### Data Analysis Key Findings\n",
        "*   A sample of 3 companies (DOW, EFX, and TFX) was successfully selected and prepared for manual review.\n",
        "*   For each company in the sample, the first 500 characters of its original transcript and its corresponding extracted topics were displayed, facilitating a side-by-side comparison.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Insights or Next Steps\n",
        "*   The next crucial step is a human-led manual review of the prepared sample to assess the precision and recall of the LLM's topic extraction and document any observed limitations or systematic errors.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": [
        "LLM Model Limitations\n",
        "Context Window Truncation (Major Recall Issue):\n",
        "\n",
        "Explanation: The most significant limitation here is our deliberate truncation of the input text to 10,000 characters (text[:10000]). LLMs have a finite context window, and we're only feeding them the beginning of a very long transcripts.\n",
        "Impact: Any important topics discussed beyond this initial segment (e.g., in later sections of the CEO's speech, financial tables, or the Q&A section) will be completely invisible to the LLM. This will severely limit the recall of our extraction, meaning we  likely missed many relevant topics present in the full document.\n",
        "Observation during review: When we read the first 1500 characters and find the extracted topics seem accurate, but if we were to skim the entire transcript, we might find other significant topics that were never considered by the LLM.\n",
        "Hallucination/Misinterpretation (Precision Issue):\n",
        "\n",
        "Explanation: LLMs, even powerful ones like Qwen3, can sometimes generate plausible-sounding but incorrect information. They might misinterpret a nuanced discussion, identify a topic that's only tangentially related, or even invent a topic that isn't truly present.\n",
        "Impact: This directly affects precision (topics identified are actually present).\n",
        "\n",
        "Sensitivity to Prompting & System Instructions:\n",
        "\n",
        "Explanation: While we've provided clear system messages and a Pydantic schema, LLMs can still be sensitive to the exact phrasing.\n",
        "\n",
        "Topic Granularity/Consistency:\n",
        "\n",
        "Explanation: The LLM might struggle to maintain consistent granularity for topics across different transcripts.  It is using different phrasing for the same underlying concept \"Financial performance and revenue growth.\"\n",
        "Impact: Makes aggregation and comparative analysis across companies more challenging.\n",
        "\n",
        "Domain Specificity (Qwen3:latest):\n",
        "\n",
        "Explanation: Qwen3:latest is a general-purpose language model. While it has broad knowledge, it hasn't been specifically fine-tuned for the nuances of financial earnings transcripts. It might miss subtle financial cues or industry-specific jargon that a specialized model might catch.\n",
        "Impact: Could lead to less insightful or less accurate topic extraction in highly specialized contexts.\n",
        "\n",
        "Informal Precision and Recall Assessment\n",
        "\n",
        "Informal Precision:\n",
        "\n",
        "It looks like based on the initial scan of the transcript the precision is about 80% based on the coverage but the topic modelling could have been better.\n",
        "\n",
        "Informal Recall is also ok, it looks like it could capture most of what needs to be captured based on the initial scanning of the transcript data but would need a better grasp at formulating the topics that would be more specific."
